gaR4(){return"Plan from the trail head"},
gaR3(){return"\u201cStart in planner\u201d opens the planner with the trail head as start and this trail picked; \u201cFinish in planner\u201d ends the route at the trail head."},
gaP2(){return"Not every ride is a loop"},
gaP1(){return"Open the planner and pick your trails as usual."},
gaP4(){return"Where does the ride end?"},
gaP3(){return"In the planner's header: tap \u201cEnd\u201d \u2014 back to the start (loop), a point on the map or the end of the last trail."},
gaQJ(){return"Where is that climb?"},
gaQI(){return"Plan a loop or a route to a trail and slide your finger across the elevation profile: the bike on the map follows, with distance and height of the spot above the profile."},
gaP6(){return"A loop from your trails"},
gaP5(){return"The button opens the planner: a rail of its own on the left, and every trail you tap on the map joins the loop \u2014 a second tap takes it out again."},
gaP8(){return"The planner\u2019s rail"},
gaP7(){return"At the top start, end and the budget with \u201cAdjust\u201d. On the left point, area and list: draw round an area, and the trails inside are in. At the bottom the bar counts what is selected and plans with one button."},
gaR8(){return"Map view and S grade"},
gaR7(){return"\u201cOn the map\u201d keeps only the trails in the map view \u2014 the list follows when you move the map. The S grade chip opens two sliders for the range. It applies on the map too."},
gaQr(){return"Rides from other apps"},
gaQq(){return"Here TrailBuddy reads GPX or zip files. In the Android app, \u201cRides for your rider profile\u201d then appears below the tracks: choose the bike, save, let it learn. The rides stay on the device."},
gaQH(){return"Avoid or don\u2019t mind"},
gaQG(){return"In the planner under \u201cSettings\u201d: roads, hiking trails uphill and steep ramps, each avoid or don\u2019t mind. Don\u2019t mind is not free \u2014 a fraction of the penalty remains. The device remembers your choice, and it applies to the route to a trail too."},
gaQP(){return"Flatter where possible"},
gaQO(){return"The planner and \u201cTo trail head\u201d count every metre of climbing above 10 % gradient extra, and the steeper, the more \u2014 on gravel and paths three times as much as on tarmac. A detour that climbs more gently then wins. Uphill trails never count as too steep."},
gaQX(){return"Heights without a recording"},
gaQW(){return"If no contribution has heights, they come from the terrain model (90 m): with reception from the map host, otherwise from your areas. The tile then carries a \u201c\u2248\u201d, the profile says so \u2014 nothing is saved."},
gaOR(){return"The line as a file"},
gaOQ(){return"Passes the trail on as a GPX file \u2014 through the share menu to any app that reads GPX. Only the line with its name; no buddies, no reports. Rides are exported under \u201cMy rides\u201d."},
gaR6(){return"The way from your areas"},
gaR5(){return"Works out the way from your position to the start of the trail \u2014 offline, from the ways in your saved areas, according to your rider profile. The map shows the line, the sheet elevation and time; as GPX it goes to any navigation app."},
gaPU(){return"To your navigation app"},
